# XSH tmpnam()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# tmpnam() is obsolescent but is still on the page, so it is tested. The
# names are only looked up (lstat, stat); no file is created.

assertion  XSH.tmpnam.001
anchor     functions/tmpnam.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/tmpnam.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       tmpnam(buf) with a buffer of L_tmpnam bytes returns buf itself, a
           non-empty string shorter than L_tmpnam, that does not name an
           existing file.
test       t/001-buffer.c

assertion  XSH.tmpnam.002
anchor     functions/tmpnam.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       tmpnam(NULL) returns a pointer to a non-empty string that does not
           name an existing file.
test       t/002-null-argument.c

assertion  XSH.tmpnam.003
anchor     functions/tmpnam.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       Twenty-five successive calls (TMP_MAX is at least 25) return
           25 different strings, copied before the next call.
test       t/003-different.c

assertion  XSH.tmpnam.004
anchor     functions/tmpnam.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       Three successive names are valid pathnames of files that do not
           exist (lstat() fails with ENOENT) and, when they have a directory
           part, that directory exists. Nothing is created.
test       t/004-creatable.c
